  "findings": [
    {
      "claim": "The judge reads a unit's files at main, as the brief says. So when a batch adds a brand-new file to a failed unit's folder, and that file falls only to the fallback surface while every main file of the unit sits under explicitly listed surfaces, the unit can count as unaffected. The batch could then get one whole repeat for a red it caused.",
      "evidence": "metasystem/cmd/metasystem/intent_landing_flake.go lists files with `ls-tree origin/main:UNIT`, and HoldingSurfaces only puts the fallback surface on files no path matches. The brief defines 'its files are the files directly in its folder at origin/main', so this follows the specification. In the real contract the case almost never comes up: packages listed file by file (for example internal/jsonedit and internal/fixtureauth) still have an unlisted testmain_test.go, so the fallback surface holds them and a new file makes them affected. Even when it does happen, a pass after the repeat is recorded and a fix goal is opened, never silently landed. Not material: the slice works and is safe without a change. This belongs on the design's 'later, when it hurts' list.",
      "id": "N-1",
      "material": false,
      "severity": "low"
    },
    {
      "claim": "The judge finds the contract file's location through the testing.contract key in metasystem.conf, which it reads from the lane's working tree (the batch's merge) rather than from main. Only the contract file itself is read at origin/main.",
      "evidence": "metasystem/cmd/metasystem/intent_landing_flake.go calls config.CommittedLookup(filepath.Join(installation, \"metasystem.conf\"), ...) and then `git show origin/main:PREFIX/RELATIVE`. The brief prescribes exactly this, following intent_landing_resolve.go:32-50. A batch would have to change the key to point at another committed contract on main, and our own agents make mistakes but nobody attacks (the default threat model). Not material.",
      "id": "N-2",
      "material": false,
      "severity": "low"
    },
